# CalAi environment variables
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values:
#     cp .env.example .env          (PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.example .env)
#
# `.env` is gitignored. This file is committed and must never contain a real
# secret — it exists so anyone cloning the repo knows exactly what to set.

# ---- Postgres (consumed by docker-compose.yml) ------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=calai
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=calai_dev_password
POSTGRES_DB=calai
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# ---- Backend ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The +asyncpg driver is required: SQLAlchemy 2.0 async needs an async driver.
# A plain postgresql:// URL here will fail at startup, on purpose, rather than
# silently blocking the event loop with a sync driver.
#
# Use 127.0.0.1, NOT localhost. On Windows, `localhost` resolves to the IPv6
# address ::1 first, but docker-compose publishes the port on IPv4 only — so
# every new connection waits for the IPv6 attempt to fail before retrying.
# Measured on this project: 2123 ms per connection via localhost, 84 ms via
# 127.0.0.1. A 25x difference that looks like "Postgres is slow" and is not.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://calai:calai_dev_password@127.0.0.1:5432/calai

ENVIRONMENT=development
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ---- Phase 1: auth ----------------------------------------------------------
# Generate a real one with:  python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"
# Changing this invalidates every issued token, which is the intended panic button.
# The app refuses to start on this placeholder value, on purpose: shipping with
# it would let anyone who has read the repo forge a token for any account.
SECRET_KEY=replace-me-with-a-64-byte-random-string
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=15
REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=30
PASSWORD_RESET_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30

# ---- Rate limiting -----------------------------------------------------------
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true

# Where the counters live. Empty means the memory of one process — correct on a
# laptop, wrong the moment more than one worker serves traffic: each keeps its
# own tally, so a limit of ten becomes ten per worker, and a restart forgives an
# attacker mid-attack. Set this and every worker shares one counter.
#
# `docker compose up -d` starts one locally. Hosted options with a free tier that
# is far more than enough for this: Upstash, Redis Cloud.
#
# If Redis goes away at runtime the limiter falls back to per-process counting
# and logs that it has done so, rather than locking every user out.
#
# REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0
REDIS_PORT=6379

# ---- Admin console -------------------------------------------------------------
# Serves the review/report console at /admin. On by default, because a queue
# nobody can action is a queue that fills up.
#
# Setting this to false removes the page only. The admin API is unchanged and
# already answers 404 to non-admins, so this is about not advertising that the
# surface exists rather than about access control.
# ADMIN_UI_ENABLED=true

# ---- Proxy -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Only enable behind a proxy that overwrites X-Forwarded-For (Railway, Fly, a
# properly configured nginx). Trusting the header without one lets any client
# forge its address and bypass every per-IP limit.
TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=false

# ---- Supabase Auth ------------------------------------------------------------
# From Project Settings -> API. Empty means Supabase Auth is off and the
# hand-built auth is still in charge; the two coexist during the migration.
#
# Neither value is a secret. The anon key is compiled into the mobile app and
# Supabase's model assumes an attacker holds it — access is controlled by token
# verification and row-level security, never by hiding this.
#
# No JWT secret is needed. The project signs with ES256 and publishes the public
# keys at <SUPABASE_URL>/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json, so the backend verifies
# with a public key and holds nothing worth stealing.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---- CORS ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser origins allowed to read responses, in production only. Development
# allows everything.
#
# Empty is a deliberate default, not an unfinished one: the only client is a
# native app, and React Native ignores CORS entirely. Sending no headers denies
# every cross-origin browser read while changing nothing for real users. Set it
# when the Expo web target is actually deployed.
#
# CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.calai.com,https://calai.com

# ---- Phase 3: nutrition data sources ----------------------------------------
# Free key from https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/api-key-signup.html
# Limit: 1,000 requests/hour. DEMO_KEY is 30/hour and 50/day — fine to smoke
# test with, useless for real development.
USDA_API_KEY=DEMO_KEY

# Open Food Facts requires a descriptive User-Agent or it will rate-limit and
# eventually ban you. Format: AppName/Version (contact email)
# Limits are severe: 15 req/min for product reads, 10 req/min for search.
OFF_USER_AGENT=CalAi/0.1 (aminezaara67@gmail.com)

# ---- AI meal photo scanning --------------------------------------------------
# Free key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey — no credit card, ~250
# requests/day on the free tier. Leave blank to disable photo scanning: the app
# still runs and the /scan endpoint answers 503 explaining how to enable it.
#
# The model only identifies foods and estimates portion sizes. Every calorie and
# macro still comes from USDA, so nothing here can introduce an invented value.
# Photo scanning spends this key.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Meal descriptions and the nutrition-estimate fallback spend this one.
#
# Two keys because the free tier is metered per key. It doubles the daily budget,
# but the real reason is isolation: with one key, describing ten meals in an
# afternoon exhausts the quota photo scanning needs — and a description can
# always be typed into search instead, while a photograph cannot.
#
# Optional. Blank falls back to GEMINI_API_KEY, so one key still works.
GEMINI_TEXT_API_KEY=

GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-flash-latest

# ---- Supabase (production) ---------------------------------------------------
# Supabase IS Postgres, so DATABASE_URL simply becomes a connection string from
# the project's Connect dialog and nothing about the app's queries changes.
#
# Supabase offers three, and picking the wrong one fails in ways that do not
# name the real cause:
#
#   Direct              db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432
#                       IPv6 ONLY unless you pay for the IPv4 add-on. On a home
#                       network without IPv6 this simply refuses to connect.
#
#   Session pooler      aws-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432      <-- USE THIS
#                       IPv4 on every tier, and each client holds its connection
#                       for the whole session, so prepared statements work.
#
#   Transaction pooler  aws-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543
#                       IPv4, but Supabase state plainly that transaction mode
#                       does not support prepared statements. asyncpg uses them
#                       for every query, so this produces intermittent
#                       "prepared statement does not exist" errors under load
#                       rather than a clean failure at startup. Only usable with
#                       statement_cache_size=0 passed through to the driver.
#
# Then two edits to whatever you copy:
#
#   1. `postgres://` or `postgresql://` must become `postgresql+asyncpg://`.
#      A sync driver is refused at startup, on purpose.
#   2. Percent-encode the password. An @ / # ? or : in it truncates the URL into
#      something that fails to parse, and the error names neither the password
#      nor the character.
#
# Note the pooler username is `postgres.<projectref>`, not plain `postgres`.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://postgres.<ref>:<encoded-password>@aws-1-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
